{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789405848,"nonce":"BCmBqjA7rbSJdI/gRIC21jS6gN4=","title":"Python: int(s) and float(s) parsing without an exception on the failure path","message":"`try: v = int(s) except ValueError: v = 0` per row is the common way\nto parse a column. The failure path raises: a cold call builds the\nexception instance and its box, sets the pending slot, and the handler\nreleases both. Fine for a real error, slow as a per-row branch.\n\nFix: parsing primitives that hand back a flag (`zb_int_parse(s) -\u003e\n(ok, value)` shape, or a sentinel plus a check), and a lowering that\nrecognises `try: x = int(s) except ValueError: \u003cfallback\u003e` with a\nhandler that keeps nothing of the exception (the analysis behind\n6907e87 already decides that) and emits the flag test in place of the\nraise and catch.","files":null}]}